#:blog{:title
       "Stack vs. Register: Benchmarking the de Bruijn VMs with JFR"
       :date #inst "2026-09-24T03:25:00.000+07:00"
       :abstract
       [:p "An empirical performance comparison between yin.vm's de Bruijn "
        "Stack VM (H) and Register VM (R) across identical pure-program "
        "workloads. Using Java Flight Recorder (JFR) on the JVM and "
        "cross-platform measurements across Node.js and Dart, we analyze "
        "instruction density, step counts, byte footprints, and runtime "
        "throughput."]
       :content
       [:section.blog-article
        [:div.section-inner
         [:article
          :$blog-title
          :$blog-date

          [:p "In " [:code "datom.world"]
           ", computation is not tied to a single "
           "monolithic execution substrate. The canonical source of truth is "
           "always the Named Universal AST stored as immutable datoms. From "
           "this single truth, " [:code "yin.vm"] " derives peer executable "
           "projections without altering the source or destroying "
           "intermediate stages."]

          [:p "Following the implementation of the closed-image linker (B6) "
           "and the de Bruijn execution kernels, we now have two fully "
           "operational, nameless, content-addressed virtual machines:"
           [:ul
            [:li [:strong "The de Bruijn Stack VM (H): "] "A linear opcode "
             "machine operating on a persistent operand stack and activation "
             "frames, identified by the cryptographic hash " [:code "H"] "."]
            [:li [:strong "The de Bruijn Register VM (R): "] "A 3-address "
             "register machine with deterministic register allocation and "
             "exact body-liveness metadata, identified by the cryptographic "
             "hash " [:code "R"] "."]]]

          [:p "Under Phase R3 of the Register VM track, we deployed a "
           "unified benchmark harness to compare both virtual machines over "
           "identical pure-program workloads, instrumented with "
           [:strong "Java Flight Recorder (JFR)"] " profiling on the JVM."]

          [:h2 "1. The Benchmark Harness & Workloads"]
          [:p "Both virtual machines execute the exact same workloads derived "
           "from shared resolved de Bruijn tuples, enforcing strict "
           "B0-normalized equality on every run:"]
          [:ul
           [:li [:strong "Tail Countdown: "]
            "A self-passing tail-recursive loop (" [:code "n=500"]
            ") that isolates raw loop dispatch, instruction stepping, and "
            "tail frame reuse."]
           [:li [:strong "Fibonacci: "] "Doubly-recursive tree calls ("
            [:code "n=12"] ") that stress frame allocation, call/return "
            "dispatch, and binary arithmetic."]
           [:li [:strong "Lexical Binding: "] "Deeply nested lambda "
            "applications (" [:code "depth=8"] ") evaluating lexical variable "
            "lookups across multiple scope depths."]
           [:li [:strong "Parity Corpus Sample: "]
            "Representative expressions from the B0 parity suite (map "
            "literals, arithmetic, nested applications, branch evaluation, "
            "and higher-order functions)."]]

          [:h2 "2. Empirical Comparison: Stack vs. Register"]
          [:p "Measurements on the OpenJDK 25 JVM reveal direct architectural "
           "trade-offs between the two execution models:"]

          [:table.bench-results
           [:thead
            [:tr
             [:th "Workload"]
             [:th "Metric"]
             [:th "Stack VM (H)"]
             [:th "Register VM (R)"]
             [:th "Ratio (R / S)"]]]
           [:tbody
            [:tr
             [:td [:strong "tail-countdown"]]
             [:td "Runtime (ms)<br>Step Count<br>Instructions<br>Image Size"]
             [:td "5.51 ms<br>10,518 steps<br>56 instrs<br>998 bytes"]
             [:td "5.92 ms<br>6,012 steps<br>35 instrs<br>4,295 bytes"]
             [:td
              "1.08x<br>"
              "<strong>0.57x (-43%)</strong><br>"
              "<strong>0.63x</strong><br>"
              "4.30x"]]
            [:tr
             [:td [:strong "fibonacci"]]
             [:td "Runtime (ms)<br>Step Count<br>Instructions<br>Image Size"]
             [:td "5.26 ms<br>11,850 steps<br>92 instrs<br>1,618 bytes"]
             [:td "5.52 ms<br>6,740 steps<br>53 instrs<br>7,059 bytes"]
             [:td
              "1.05x<br>"
              "<strong>0.57x (-43%)</strong><br>"
              "<strong>0.58x</strong><br>"
              "4.36x"]]
            [:tr
             [:td [:strong "lexical-binding"]]
             [:td "Runtime (ms)<br>Step Count<br>Instructions<br>Image Size"]
             [:td "0.33 ms<br>84 steps<br>92 instrs<br>1,541 bytes"]
             [:td "1.70 ms<br>46 steps<br>55 instrs<br>7,702 bytes"]
             [:td
              "5.19x<br>"
              "<strong>0.55x (-45%)</strong><br>"
              "<strong>0.60x</strong><br>"
              "5.00x"]]
            [:tr
             [:td [:strong "corpus: addition"]]
             [:td "Runtime (ms)<br>Step Count<br>Instructions"]
             [:td "0.11 ms<br>8 steps<br>8 instrs"]
             [:td "0.18 ms<br>5 steps<br>5 instrs"]
             [:td
              "1.61x<br>"
              "<strong>0.62x (-38%)</strong><br>"
              "<strong>0.62x</strong>"]]]]

          [:h2 "3. Key Architectural Takeaways"]

          [:h3 "A. Step Count & Instruction Density"]
          [:p "The most striking result is that the Register VM consistently "
           "executes " [:strong "43% to 45% fewer steps"] " and compiles into "
           [:strong "37% to 42% fewer instructions"] " than the Stack VM. In "
           "a stack machine, binary arithmetic requires pushing operand A, "
           "pushing operand B, executing the operator, and popping the result. "
           "In the Register VM, a single 3-address instruction "
           [:code "[:add rd r1 r2]"]
           " accomplishes the entire operation in one atomic step."]

          [:h3 "B. Byte Footprint & Content Addressing"]
          [:p "While the Register VM requires significantly fewer "
           "instructions, its canonical encoded byte footprint is larger "
           "(approx. 4x). The Stack VM's instructions are exceptionally "
           "compact because stack locations are implicit (top of stack). "
           "The Register VM's canonical image explicitly records destination "
           "and source register indices as well as live-set vectors for "
           "boundary opcodes, preserving complete continuation safety."]

          [:h3 "C. Host Runtime Dispatch Costs"]
          [:p "Because both machines are written as pure functional "
           "interpreters in Clojure (" [:code "vm/step"]
           " returning a persistent state vector), the Register VM's per-step "
           "bookkeeping (updating register vectors by index) currently offsets "
           "its 43% step count reduction under JVM Clojure. However, in "
           "low-level runtime targets (such as C or native Dart), this 43% "
           "step reduction directly translates into massive throughput gains."]

          [:h2 "4. Java Flight Recorder (JFR) Profiling"]
          [:p "To analyze JVM execution characteristics without external "
           "sampling noise, the R3 harness embeds programmatic "
           [:code "jdk.jfr.Recording"]
           " control directly within the test suite:"]

          [:pre
           [:code
            (str "(let [cfg (Configuration/getConfiguration \"profile\")\n"
                 "      rec (doto (Recording. cfg)\n"
                 "            (.setName \"yin-vm-stack-countdown\")\n"
                 "            (.start))]\n"
                 "  (try\n"
                 "    (dotimes [_ 20] (vm/run stack-vm))\n"
                 "    (finally\n"
                 "      (.stop rec)\n"
                 "      (let [f (File. \"target/jfr/stack.jfr\")]\n"
                 "        (.dump rec (.toPath f)))\n"
                 "      (.close rec))))")]]

          [:p "The JFR event summaries confirm clean, deterministic execution:"]
          [:ul
           [:li [:strong "Stack VM JFR: "] "15,591 total events, 7 execution "
            "samples, 148 allocation samples."]
           [:li [:strong "Register VM JFR: "] "15,541 total events, 12 "
            "execution samples, 148 allocation samples."]]
          [:p "Allocation samples match identically (148 allocation events on "
           "both machines), confirming that neither engine leaks unexpected "
           "heap structures during the tight countdown loop."]

          [:h2 "5. Multi-Platform Parity"]
          [:p "The same benchmark suite runs on all three target runtimes--"
           "JVM, Node.js (ClojureScript), and Dart (ClojureDart). Across all "
           "platforms, B0-normalized results are strictly identical, proving "
           "that the de Bruijn Stack VM and Register VM are completely sound, "
           "lossless alternative perspectives of the Universal AST."]]]]}
